# Bitcraftly Performance Standards

Apply these standards whenever implementing or modifying performance-sensitive code.

This rule complements:

- Bitcraftly-Engineering-Standards.mdc
- Bitcraftly-Architecture-Protection-Rules.mdc
- Bitcraftly-Accessibility-Standards.mdc

---

# Performance Goals

Every implementation should aim to:

- Minimize JavaScript shipped to the browser.
- Prefer server-side rendering when appropriate.
- Reduce unnecessary rendering.
- Keep bundles as small as possible.
- Maintain excellent Core Web Vitals.
- Avoid performance regressions.

---

# Next.js Standards

Prefer:

- Server Components
- Streaming
- Route-level code splitting
- Dynamic imports for heavy components
- Metadata API
- Optimized fonts
- Optimized images
- Static rendering whenever possible

Avoid:

- Unnecessary Client Components
- Client-side rendering by default
- Blocking rendering with unnecessary JavaScript

---

# React Standards

Prefer:

- Small focused components
- Composition
- Suspense where appropriate
- Lazy loading for large components

Avoid:

- Deep component trees
- Large monolithic components
- Excessive prop drilling

---

# Rendering

Minimize unnecessary rendering.

Avoid:

- unnecessary state
- duplicated state
- unnecessary useEffect
- unnecessary useMemo
- unnecessary useCallback

Use memoization only when measurable performance benefits exist.

---

# Data Fetching

Prefer:

- Server-side fetching
- Parallel requests
- Cached requests
- Incremental revalidation when appropriate

Avoid:

- Sequential request waterfalls
- Duplicate API calls
- Client-side fetching unless required

---

# Bundle Optimization

Always:

- Remove dead code
- Remove unused imports
- Prefer tree-shakeable libraries
- Dynamically import heavy modules
- Avoid unnecessary dependencies

Never add a dependency when existing project utilities solve the problem.

---

# Images

Use Next.js Image component whenever possible.

Always:

- Specify width and height
- Use responsive sizes
- Optimize large assets
- Lazy load non-critical images

Avoid oversized images.

---

# Fonts

Prefer:

- next/font
- Variable fonts
- Font subsets

Avoid render-blocking fonts.

---

# CSS

Prefer:

- Tailwind utilities
- Shared design tokens
- Minimal custom CSS

Avoid:

- duplicated styles
- unused classes
- excessive nesting

---

# Animations

Animations should:

- remain smooth
- avoid layout thrashing
- respect prefers-reduced-motion

Prefer:

- transform
- opacity

Avoid animating layout properties.

---

# Lists

Large collections should:

- paginate
- virtualize when appropriate
- lazy render

Avoid rendering very large lists unnecessarily.

---

# Core Web Vitals

Protect:

- LCP
- CLS
- INP
- FCP
- TTFB

Never introduce avoidable regressions.

---

# AI Behaviour

When generating code:

- Prefer performance-friendly patterns.
- Prefer Server Components.
- Prefer existing optimized components.
- Do not optimize prematurely.
- Do not sacrifice readability for micro-optimizations.
- Explain significant performance trade-offs.

---

# Verification Checklist

Before completing a task verify:

- No unnecessary Client Components introduced.
- No unnecessary JavaScript shipped.
- Bundle size remains reasonable.
- Images are optimized.
- Data fetching is efficient.
- Existing performance is preserved.

---

# Definition of Done

Performance requirements are satisfied when:

- No measurable regressions are introduced.
- Rendering remains efficient.
- Existing caching strategy is preserved.
- Core Web Vitals are not negatively affected.
- Bundle size has not unnecessarily increased.